  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 AM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nIn the upper levels, a ridge is over the south and Midwest today. A \ntrough is over the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic. The northwest flow \npattern continues with storms forming upstream in the Midwest then \nriding the ridge down through Kentucky and into Southwest Virgina \nand Northeast Tennessee. This afternoon and early evening showers \nand storms will be isolated in the south and more scattered to the \nnorth. A minor wave will move through the Ohio Valley this evening \nbringing storms a little farther north into West Virginia. The best \nchance for storms this afternoon and evening remains in Southwest \nVirginia and Northeast Tennessee. SPC brings the marginal risk to \naround the Knoxville area for today and the slight risk clips our \nnortheastern-most counties in Southwest Virginia and Northeast \nTennessee. Flash flooding will continue to be a concern especially \nin Southwest Virginia and Northeast Tennessee where soils are \nsaturated. At the moment confidence is not high enough for a flood \nwatch with some guidance bringing the bulk of the storms to our \nnorth and east.\n\nHeat indices will be high today especially in the Southern Tennessee \nValley and Plateau. A Heat Advisory has been issued for those \ncounties for this afternoon where heat indices are expected to reach \n105 degrees or higher. By Sunday and Monday, the Heat Advisory will \nlikely need to be expanded northward into the Knoxville area.\n\nOn Sunday, showers and storms will be more isolated as the ridge \nflattens and we break free from the northwest flow pattern. Even \nwith a decrease in storm activity, there is still a possibility \nstrong winds or isolated flooding in the storms that do form.\n\nBy Monday, the ridge weakens and the trough dips into the Tennessee \nValley. A decaying cold front will plunge southward on Monday. As a \nresult, the best chances of rain will be Monday with the best \ncoverage in the northeast half of the region. By mid week, a ridge \nwill move back over the Tennessee Valley. Tuesday and Wednesday will \nbe mostly dry and late in the week POPs look typical for this time \nof year with mostly diurnal convection.",
